        "lang": "en",
        "note_tweet": {
          "text": "Traces just joined Vercel today. Here's what you need to know.\n\nTarun Sachdeva, founder of Traces, announced it on X: \"Some big news today: Traces is joining Vercel!\" The post pulled about 47.5K views within hours. The Traces account called it a very important company update.\n\nTraces is a Toronto company. It launched in February as a CLI and sharing network for AI coding agent runs. You point it at Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI or Pi, and it records the session end to end so teams can save and share it.\n\nIts core idea builds on git. A commit hook adds a note linking each commit to the agent session that made it. Teammates reviewing the code can open that session and see how it was produced.\n\nVercel CEO Guillermo Rauch says Traces are the essential new primitive of software development and agentic evolution. His argument is that git changed the world by helping people collaborate around the delta unit of code. Traces go further, because they capture the upstream intent, prompts, reasoning and skills behind the code.\n\nOthers in the community made the same point. A diff only shows what changed, while a trace shows why the agent made the change. Traces can also serve as eval data, since each human correction of an agent is recorded. And intent makes it easier to spot something like a hidden backdoor. Vercel's Malte Ubl and other Vercel staff welcomed the team.\n\nThis fits Vercel's recent push on tracing. Vercel Tracing became generally available on all plans on Nov 6, and lets you search and inspect traces from the dashboard or the Vercel CLI.\n\nKey numbers:\n- Announced Nov 17, 2026\n- Traces launched in February, so about 9 months as an independent company\n- Supports 5 agents: Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Pi\n- Original post: about 47.5K views, 525 likes\n- Deal terms were not disclosed\n\nTraces is now part of Vercel, and the team will keep building there."
        },
